/target

# `Cargo.lock` is deliberately *not* ignored.
#
# Cargo's rule is: ignore it for a library, commit it for a binary. This
# workspace ships binaries — `grasm`, and the runtime a `--native` build links
# against — so the lock file is what makes a build of tag `v1.0.0` today the
# same build in a year. Ignoring it meant CI resolved fresh versions on every
# run, which is the opposite of what a release tag promises.
.DS_Store
__pycache__/

# The security benchmark builds a throwaway Rust project to prove `std::fs` is
# ambient there. Its lock file is an artifact of running the gate, not source.
/benchmarks/security/rust/app/Cargo.lock

# Local editor and tooling state is never source. Whatever your tools write
# into the working tree -- caches, session files, automation logs -- belongs
# in your own global ignore file rather than this one, which describes the
# project and not the machine it is built on.

# The MCP SDK, which `scripts/rmcptest.sh` drives the server in
# `packages/rmcp` against. This one is not machine state -- the gate needs it
# to run at all -- but it stays out of the tree on purpose: an oracle kept in
# the repo can be edited until it agrees with us, which is the one thing an
# oracle must not do. The gate says how to install it.
.mcpvenv/
